From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: x <x@y>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 01:59:45 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] CUDA: collapse the LFM shortconv decode chain into one kernel

Port of the R9700/HIP track patch 0010 to the GB10 CUDA series. The kernel and
the detector live in ggml/src/ggml-cuda, which the HIP backend shares, so the
body transfers unchanged; the one CUDA-specific change is the arithmetic fence's
inline-asm constraint, "v" (an AMDGPU VGPR constraint) becoming "f" under
__CUDA_ARCH__.

At decode the LFM2.5 shortconv block spends four kernel launches on about 80 KB
of data, in a strictly serial chain:

    MUL(b, x) -> TRANSPOSE -> CONCAT(conv_window, bx)
                                 |-> CPY(trailing cols) -> conv-state cache
                                 `-> SSM_CONV(., conv_w) -> MUL(c, .)

22 of LFM2.5's 30 layers are shortconv layers, so that is 88 dispatches per
decoded token spent on launch latency and redundant staging traffic: the window
is copied into the concat buffer, the trailing columns are copied back out to the
cache, and ssm_conv then reads the copy.

This collapses all four into ONE kernel. A thread per channel reads its conv
window and its b/x pair, multiplies, runs the stock accumulation loop, writes the
new window straight back to the cache in place and applies the c-gate. Detection
runs at the b*x MUL (the earliest node at which every input is live) and marks
the other four nodes done; the launch is gated on the decode shape (n_tokens == 1,
one sequence), so prefill keeps the stock nodes.

Bit-identity is by construction:

  - the accumulation is the stock loop nest over the same three values in the
    same order, including the `sumf += bias` add with the zero bias register;
  - b*x reaches the accumulator through an arithmetic fence. In stock it is
    stored to memory and reloaded, and a load is an opaque value that
    x[nc-1] * w[nc-1] cannot be reassociated into;
  - the writeback reads its window into registers before storing, so it is
    alias-safe when the state source IS the cache slot.

Hoisting legality is the whole correctness story. The fold issues both of its
stores at the b*x node, up to ~20 nodes earlier than the CPY and the gate MUL it
replaces. ggml-alloc reuses blocks across disjoint lifetimes, so an early store
can land on a block a node in between still reads. The detector therefore
requires both destinations to be disjoint from every input and output of every
node that stays in the window.

Measured on GB10 (sm_121, CUDA 13.0, LFM2.5-2.6B-Q4_K_M) against a build of the
13-patch parent series, arms alternated:

  - census, per decoded token: shortconv_fold_f32 fires 21 times; ssm_conv_f32,
    concat_cont and cpy_scalar each fall from 22 launches to 1, and the b*x
    k_bin_bcast from 44 to 2. Total dispatches per token 448.7 -> 342.7.
  - decode tg128, isolated on one binary with GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_SHORTCONV_FOLD:
    120.74 -> 121.54, +0.66%. With the preceding recurrent-state view patch the
    pair measures +1.64%, because that patch moves the conv window into the
    persistent cache and the fold's disjointness test then passes on 21 of 22
    layers instead of a minority of them.
  - perplexity bit-identical on both shapes, 3 fresh loads per arm: 22.7466 at
    the gate shape (-c 512 --chunks 8), 22.6048 on the decode path
    (-b 512 -ub 1 --chunks 8).

Why the gain is smaller here than the +4.2% the same patch measures on RDNA4:
the marginal cost of one dispatch inside a replayed CUDA graph on GB10 was
measured at 0.88 us (empty kernels appended to the captured decode graph,
N = 0/500/2000, slope linear at 0.85-0.96 us across segments), against 2.08 us on
the R9700. Programmatic dependent launch is on by default here and already hides
most of these bodies behind the matvecs, so what remains to recover is the
serial dispatch latency and the staging round trips, not the kernel time.

GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_SHORTCONV_FOLD=1 restores the stock nodes.
